\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The planning quantity, per condition. FRR is the verifier's false-rejection rate on baseline-correct tasks; its $95\%$ UCB is the one-sided Clopper--Pearson limit, computed on the task-level indicator ``rejects in at least one seed'' so that it is conservative with respect to within-task correlation and stays valid when no event is observed. Breakage is the observed rate for the same workflow, and slack is FRR minus breakage --- how much equation~\ref {eq:bound} over-predicts. A deployment tolerating breakage $\epsilon $ enables refinement only where the UCB column, not the FRR column, falls below $\epsilon $.}}{6}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:frr}{{1}{6}{The planning quantity, per condition. FRR is the verifier's false-rejection rate on baseline-correct tasks; its $95\%$ UCB is the one-sided Clopper--Pearson limit, computed on the task-level indicator ``rejects in at least one seed'' so that it is conservative with respect to within-task correlation and stays valid when no event is observed. Breakage is the observed rate for the same workflow, and slack is FRR minus breakage --- how much \eqref {eq:bound} over-predicts. A deployment tolerating breakage $\epsilon $ enables refinement only where the UCB column, not the FRR column, falls below $\epsilon $}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Frozen test splits, 150 tasks per family, three execution seeds. Baselines are the strongest single-call structure per family (direct prompting for code, chain-of-thought for math). \emph  {Vanilla} is generate$\to $verify$\to $refine with up to three iterations; \emph  {protected} is the same loop with a temperature-zero incumbent replaced only on verifier rejection. Intervals are stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CIs. The \texttt  {unseen} (\$0.15) budget behaves between the two shown and is reported in Appendix~\ref {app:floor}.}}{7}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:parti}{{2}{7}{Frozen test splits, 150 tasks per family, three execution seeds. Baselines are the strongest single-call structure per family (direct prompting for code, chain-of-thought for math). \emph {Vanilla} is generate$\to $verify$\to $refine with up to three iterations; \emph {protected} is the same loop with a temperature-zero incumbent replaced only on verifier rejection. Intervals are stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CIs. The \texttt {unseen} (\$0.15) budget behaves between the two shown and is reported in Appendix~\ref {app:floor}}{table.2}{}}
